{"api_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/openxla/xla/issues/45222","collected_at":"2026-09-27T21:56:52.061765+00:00","comments":[{"author_association":"CONTRIBUTOR","body":"Opened #46248 for this. Your analysis pinned it exactly — I just turned `bwd_recv_id` into a disjunction over every element of `back_pairs` instead of `back_pairs.back().second`.\n\nWorth noting for reviewers that `ForwardCycleMultipleCycles` asserted the buggy shape (a single `constant(1)` and one compare), so the existing test encoded the behaviour rather than catching it; that expectation is updated in the PR. The single-cycle tests are untouched, since with one back edge the loop runs once and emits identical HLO.\n\nI wasn't able to build XLA locally, so it rests on CI.","created_at":"2026-07-27T06:21:52Z","html_url":"https://github.com/openxla/xla/issues/45222#issuecomment-5087974484","id":5087974484,"issue_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/openxla/xla/issues/45222","minimized":null,"node_id":"IC_kwDOHyx1PM8AAAABL0RUVA","performed_via_github_app":null,"pin":null,"reactions":{"+1":0,"-1":0,"confused":0,"eyes":0,"heart":0,"hooray":0,"laugh":0,"rocket":0,"total_count":0,"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/openxla/xla/issues/comments/5087974484/reactions"},"updated_at":"2026-07-27T06:21:52Z","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/openxla/xla/issues/comments/5087974484","user":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/161562995?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Ashutosh0x/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Ashutosh0x/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Ashutosh0x/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Ashutosh0x/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/Ashutosh0x","id":161562995,"login":"Ashutosh0x","node_id":"U_kgDOCaFBcw","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Ashutosh0x/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Ashutosh0x/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Ashutosh0x/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Ashutosh0x/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Ashutosh0x/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/Ashutosh0x","user_view_type":"public"}}],"issue":{"active_lock_reason":null,"assignee":null,"assignees":[],"author_association":"CONTRIBUTOR","body":"### Summary\n`CollectivePermuteCycleDecomposer` miscompiles a `collective-permute` whose `source_target_pairs` contain **more than one** cycle: every back-edge receiver except one is routed to the forward collective-permute (in which it is not a target) and therefore outputs zeros instead of its permuted data.\n\n### Details\n`DecomposeCollectivePermuteCycle` (`xla/backends/gpu/transforms/collectives/collective_permute_cycle_decomposer.cc`) splits the permute into a backward and a forward `collective-permute` and recombines them with\n\n```\nselect(compare(partition-or-replica-id, bwd_recv_id), back_cp, fwd_cp)\n```\n\nwhere (line ~232)\n\n```cpp\nint64_t bwd_recv_id = back_pairs.back().second;\n```\n\nuses only the **last** back edge's target. For a single cycle there is exactly one back edge, so this is correct. But `GetCycleTypeAndIndices` returns multiple back-edge indices for a permute containing several disjoint cycles, and `DecomposeCollectivePermuteCycle` accumulates all of them into `back_pairs` (lines ~197-203). A single scalar-equality predicate cannot express membership in the multi-element set of back-edge targets, so only `back_pairs.back().second` selects `back_cp`; every other back-edge target selects `fwd_cp`, where it is not a target, and per collective-permute semantics a non-target outputs zeros.\n\n### Reproduction (the pass's own multi-cycle test)\n`ForwardCycleMultipleCycles` uses `{{0,2},{2,4},{4,6},{6,0},{1,3},{3,5},{5,7},{7,1}}` (two disjoint 4-cycles). The heuristic yields back-edge indices `{3,7}` → `back_pairs = {{6,0},{7,1}}`, so `bwd_recv_id = 1`. Device 0 is the target of back edge `{6,0}` and must receive device 6's value, but `id == 1` is false for device 0, so it selects `fwd_cp` (forward targets `{2,4,6,3,5,7}`, which do not include 0) and receives zeros. The test only `FileCheck`s the HLO structure (`constant(1)`, `compare(replica-id, %c1)`), not numeric output, so the miscompile is not caught.\n\n### Reachability\n`RunImpl` processes every `kCollectivePermute`; `GetCycleTypeAndIndicesArray` filters only on operand/tuple/byte-size/size and forwards multi-cycle permutes. The pass is in the GPU collectives pipeline gated behind `xla_gpu_enable_pipelined_p2p` (default off, user-settable). Multi-cycle permutes arise from SPMD partitioning of multi-axis device meshes (independent rings per mesh-axis index — the classic torus/ICI pattern), so this is reachable when the flag is enabled.\n\n### Suggested fix\nBuild the select predicate as a disjunction over **all** distinct back-edge targets — OR together `compare(id, t, EQ)` for each `t` in `{p.second : p in back_pairs}` — instead of using only `back_pairs.back().second`. The single-cycle path degenerates to the current single compare, so there is no change for the common case. (A conservative alternative is to skip decomposition when `back_pairs.size() > 1` until multi-cycle routing is implemented.)\n\nI'm happy to send a PR for the disjunction approach — flagging it as an issue first because the correct fix changes generated HLO and I can't build XLA locally to validate the numeric result on a GPU, so I'd value a maintainer's steer on the preferred direction (full multi-cycle support vs. conservatively bailing).\n","closed_at":null,"closed_by":null,"comments":1,"comments_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/openxla/xla/issues/45222/comments","created_at":"2026-07-03T21:04:56Z","events_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/openxla/xla/issues/45222/events","html_url":"https://github.com/openxla/xla/issues/45222","id":4806300129,"